## The Bridge Between Frontend and Backend

1. The Network Bridge (Security Groups)
Your Next.js Server Components are running inside your housie-cluster-dev ECS tasks. To let them talk to the backend, you must configure the Internal ALB Security Group to accept inbound HTTP/HTTPS traffic where the source is your Next.js Security Group (web-app-sg). This creates a secure, private tunnel between the two layers.

2. The Internal Address (Route 53)
When the Next.js Server Component fires a fetch() request, it needs to know where to send it. Instead of sending it out to the public internet (api.housie.us), it sends it to the Internal ALB. You map a clean internal domain name (like api.internal.housie.local) to your Internal ALB using an Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zone.


## Differnet Calls from Next.js Server Components

. Direct Browser → Backend API Calls
In Next.js, these are calls made inside useEffect or via libraries like SWR or TanStack Query.

User Interactions: Clicking "Favorite" on a listing or "Contact Agent." The browser talks directly to our API.

Client-Side Navigation: If you are already on the site and click a new link, Next.js often fetches just the JSON data from the browser to update the page without a full reload.

Polling/WebSockets: Checking for new "Price Dropped" alerts while the user has the tab open.

Auth Checks: Validating a session token directly against an auth provider (like Firebase or Auth0) from the client side.

2. Frontend Container → Backend (Server-Side)
These calls happen inside our Node.js container before the user sees anything. This is the "hidden" traffic.

A. Server Components & getServerSideProps
When a user hits a URL (e.g., [housie.com/homes/123-main-st](https://housie.com/homes/123-main-st)), the Frontend Container wakes up and calls our Backend API (or Database) to get the house details.

Why: So the page arrives in the browser fully populated with data. This is essential for SEO (Google needs to see the house price) and performance (no "loading spinners").

B. Next.js API Routes (/pages/api or /app/api)
If our frontend container has its own API routes, it acts as a Proxy.

The Flow: Browser → Frontend Container (API Route) → Actual Backend.

Why:

Hiding Secrets: we need to call a maps API (like Google Maps) but don't want our private API key visible in the browser.

CORS Bypass: If our backend is on a different domain and has strict security, the Frontend Container can talk to it more easily than a browser can.